1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Okay, so we are back at the screen. We are
now on video 30 of the professional
trading masterclass video series. We
continue with our trade idea generation
process. And now we are going into the
world of qualitative processing. So
welcome to qualitative processing one.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Now, before we get going into qualitative
analysis, let's have a little reminder of
the context here. So in terms of
fundamental analysis, the evolution of the
quantitative and qualitative process. So
first of all, we have our quantitative
phase, which splits into two areas, our
quant discovery phase and our quant
evidence phase.

3
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:16,000
In quant discovery, we are relying on
forward looking metrics and valuation. We
identify positive and negative outliers
and everything else in between, such as
turnarounds or potential value traps.

4
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:39,000
In the quant evidence phase. In the quant
evidence phase, we look to our backwards
looking financial statements. And we ask
the question, do the financial statements
provide evidence to support valuation of
the positive and negative outliers that we
identified and discovered using our
forward looking metrics?

5
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And then we move on to qualitative
analysis. And then we move on to
qualitative analysis. And what we focus on
is qualitative drivers of the business. So
we look at backwards and forwards looking
key performance indicators and the
management operating plan of the company.
So we identify the KPIs and the management
operating plan. And we ask the question,
do they provide evidence to support the
value of the business? And then we move on
to our qualitative analysis. And then we
move on to our qualitative analysis. And
what we focus on is qualitative drivers of
the business.

6
00:01:46,000 --> 00:02:15,000
What we focus on is qualitative drivers of
the business. So we focus on qualitative
drivers of the business. So we look at
backwards and forwards looking key
performance indicators and the management
operating plan of the company. So we
identify the KPIs and the management
operating plan. And we ask the question,
do they provide evidence to support the
valuation of our positive and negative
outliers that we have identified in the
quant phase?

7
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:42,000
So at the stage when we reach qualitative
analysis, what has happened is, is that
we've discovered an outlier and we've
understood the supporting quantitative
evidence from the financial statements.
And as we've done that, we have probably
learned some of the basic information
about a stock.

8
00:02:42,000 --> 00:03:08,780
So it's product and or service. So by
default, it's kind of unavoidable that
even when we're doing the backwards
looking quantitative process, where we're
looking at financial statements, we are by
default going to pick up some qualitative
information that is essentially part of a
qualitative process.

9
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:36,000
So this is where the quantitative
processes overlap. So there is overlap
between the quant and qualitative
processes at this juncture, because we are
continually asking the questions, and we
are continually looking for evidence to
support the valuation of the outlier that
we have identified.

10
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:55,000
So the next level quantitative and next
level quantitative and next level
qualitative, we are continuously seeking
evidence that gives us the full story and
the full picture in the stock, behind
valuation. And this involves both
backwards and forwards looking metrics.

11
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:22,000
When we move into the qualitative metrics.
When we move into the qualitative phase,
the first thing we are looking to do is to
identify key performance indicators of the
company. So in the quant phase, we used
forward looking valuation and growth
metrics to discover what the market likes
and dislikes in a sector and everything
else in between.

12
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,000
We also looked to the backwards, we also
looked to the backwards quantitative
financial statements for evidence to
support the outlier status of a stock that
we have discovered.

13
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:48,000
We now move on to the next stage, which is
the identification of a company's KPIs and
what the management operating plan of the
company actually is.

14
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The first question is, why in the hell are
KPIs so important to us?

15
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Well, when you see the profit and loss
account for a quarter or year end for a
particular company, it is a statement of
the results of the company.

16
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:15,000
By its very nature, it's backwards
looking. So they are the results from the
last quarter or the last year.

17
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,000
But there's something behind the results.
It's called a business.

18
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Beneath the end result, there is something
that creates the result.

19
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:38,000
So there's a business and this business is
driven by the management operating plan
and it's measured by key performance
indicators, KPIs.

20
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:45,000
So the management operating plan drives
the results of the business.

21
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:57,000
However, it's the KPIs that measure the
effectiveness of the management operating
plan and the business that's behind
creating the results.

22
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Now, unfortunately, there is no resource
where the management of a company
literally tell you line for line what
their management operating plan is.

23
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:26,000
What we have to do as traders is we have
to identify a company's KPIs and then
infer what the management operating plan
is by putting the pieces all together.

24
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
So this is what we're going to do in this
video for you with an example.

25
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:38,000
And then you can go off and just repeat
this process and use it as a template.

26
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:45,000
But first, we have to make some
assumptions. So we have a backdrop and we
have context.

27
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So for the purposes of this exercise,
we're going to assume that you have found
what you think is a good quantitative
study.

28
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:03,000
You have a quantitative stock. So you've
been through the identification process
and the backwards looking quant.

29
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:17,000
So the financial statements and the stock
that you're looking at as a potential long
idea as increasing revenue and or earnings
growth relative to its peer group.

30
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And the backdrop is that its valuation is
premium or superior to the sector.

31
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Your next job then. And by default, you
will probably find out this information
anyway when you're looking at evidence in
the financial statements and trying to
understand what drives the valuation.

32
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:49,000
So you're going to have to find out during
that process what the company actually
does and has done in the past.

33
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:58,000
And also where you think the company is
potentially going in the future.

34
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:16,000
So we're also going to make the assumption
here that you've looked at all of the past
info on the company and now you know what
they do and what probably sets their
product and or service sets it apart from
the competition.

35
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Now, some things we have to remember as
well as in terms of best discipline.

36
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:31,000
So remember, there will always be a reason
why the quant was good.

37
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:41,000
There is a reason why earnings growth and
or revenue growth is forecasted by the
market to be better than average of the
peer group.

38
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:54,000
There's also a reason why the market rates
the company higher via a higher than
average price earnings ratio and peg
ratio.

39
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:05,000
So there is a reason why people are
relatively putting dollars into the stock
at the expense of other similar
businesses.

40
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:27,000
There's also a reason why the market, the
market, the market, the market, the market
wants to own the stock at the expense of
other stocks that have inferior products
or services.

41
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:35,000
So the company is probably taking market
share in the sector.

42
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:46,000
Now, this phase where we are learning
about a stock, we have to understand it
always in the context of what constitutes
a trade idea.

43
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:58,000
So the first step when we're learning
about a stock is always to go to the
investors relations website of the company
and we start hoarding qualitative
information.

44
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So we continue to learn what the company
does and why they are perceived to be
superior to their competition or peer
group.

45
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,000
What this is, is learning about the
company.

46
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
But don't forget, this is not generating a
trade idea.

47
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Just simply learning about what a company
does and why it has a superior valuation,
what its product and service is.

48
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
That isn't a trade idea.

49
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:47,000
There's a big difference between learning
about the company, what it does and why it
is potentially superior versus learning
about whether the stock will actually go
up in the next 60 days.

50
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Don't confuse the two exercises.

51
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
This is a big error amongst retail
traders.

52
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:02,000
A lot of people have the ability to go
through some processes and identify what a
good company is.

53
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
But a good company does not mean a good
stock.

54
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
So a trade idea is not an investment idea.

55
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:18,000
We are not in this business to look for
good companies and then just buy the stock
and hold forever.

56
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
We are traders.

57
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Catalysts are required.

58
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
And of course, we are going to come on to
catalysts later.

59
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:39,000
So as we've been through that process of
learning about a stock, we have to
understand that we are learning the
information of the stock.

60
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:46,000
And the information alone doesn't qualify
it as a trade idea.

61
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:51,000
We are in the evidence gathering phase.

62
00:11:51,000 --> 00:12:01,000
And obviously, that is a qualitative
process because we are gathering
qualitative information.

63
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:12,000
When we move into our qualitative
analysis, when we are looking at KPIs,
this is how we try to predict the future.

64
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:27,000
And this is done once we know the past and
we are fairly confident or very confident
that we know why the company has a
superior rating or better product or
service.

65
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:54,000
So the question to keep in mind when we
are going through the qualitative process,
when we are evidence gathering on forward
looking qualitative information, when we
are looking at KPIs and the management
operating plan, that question always is
how does the company's future look
compared to expectations?

66
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
So as mentioned previously, before we
looked at our backdrop of assumptions.

67
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:13,000
So as mentioned previously, before we
looked at our backdrop of assumptions, the
first part of the qualitative phase is to
identify what key performance indicators
are actually important to the company.

68
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Now, back in the quantitative phase where
we looked at the financial statements of a
company for evidence to support our
forward looking valuations and growth
rates, we will have gone to the investor
relations website of the company to get
the financial statements.

69
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Now, the investor relations websites of
public companies are incredibly useful in
the qualitative phase of the trade idea
generation process.

70
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Why?

71
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Because public companies have to, by order
of regulation, update investors
continuously on the state of their
business.

72
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:13,000
And they do this, obviously, through their
quarterly and annual earnings releases, of
course, but they also do this with verbal
and written communication in between
earnings releases.

73
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:25,000
So companies can choose to do the minimum
where they just release their quarterly
earnings reports or they can choose to do
more than the minimum.

74
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:38,000
So they can be very inactive with their
communication in between earnings releases
or they can actually be highly active
between earnings releases and engaging.

75
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:45,000
Now, we're going to come on to this a bit
later in the video series in the section
on catalysts.

76
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:57,000
At this stage of the process, what we are
seeking to identify is what drives the
business in a qualitative capacity beneath
the end result,

77
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
which is the hard numbers in the financial
statements.

78
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:07,000
So we're looking at the qualitative
elements of the business that drive the
quantitative results.

79
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:23,000
And the investor relations websites of
companies can provide us with an absolute
treasure trove of information to identify
key performance indicators and the
management operating plan.

80
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Now, whenever we go to investor relations
websites and we are going through this
process of identifying KPIs, we need to
ask ourselves continuous questions.

81
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:51,000
So again, we are assuming here that you've
been through the quant process, you've
identified your outlier, you've been
through the backwards looking quant,
learning the stock info,

82
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and then we're now going into the
qualitative phase.

83
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:07,000
So we have to keep viewing our work
through a particular lens in the same way
that we did during our previous processes.

84
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:15,000
So we need to keep asking ourselves the
question, why is earnings growth or
revenue growth so good or bad?

85
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Why does the market value the company in
the way that it does?

86
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Does the stock deserve to be on its
assigned premium or discount?

87
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Will the company continue to report good
numbers or bad numbers as the case may be?

88
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:42,000
Beating expectations or missing
expectations as the case may be in revenue
and earnings?

89
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:45,000
Is this a high probability?

90
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:54,000
And we answer all of these questions via
the key performance indicators and the
management operating plan.

91
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
This is how companies measure themselves.

92
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:01,000
So how they give guidance to the market.

93
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:11,000
And it's how analysts measure the
company's performance to enable them to
forecast revenue and earnings outcomes.

94
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:18,000
So this is how analysts get their
expectations for revenue and earnings.

95
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Now, we are going to go through now an
example of how to identify KPIs, key
performance indicators.

96
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:33,000
And the example that we're going to look
at is Zendesk.

97
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:45,000
And when we arrive at the qualitative
phase, we should have at least a basic
understanding of what the company actually
does.

98
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,000
So what does Zendesk do?

99
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:57,000
It is a customer relationship management,
a CRM software as a service SaaS company.

100
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:03,000
It is a business to business product and
it serves business to consumer businesses.

101
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And its core function is helping to solve
customer issues faster.

102
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:14,000
So it gives a help desk platform to
businesses.

103
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:36,000
So what the software actually does, it
helps businesses in management of customer
communication through various channels
like email, web forms, chat, voice calls
and social media like Twitter, Facebook
and LinkedIn using an omni channel
ticketing system.

104
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:49,000
The software also has automation of
customer support processing and they're
utilizing various macros and other
automation tools.

105
00:18:49,000 --> 00:19:04,000
And they also have customer self-service
tools that utilize bots for auto replies,
there's knowledge bases, there's customer
communities and FAQs, frequently asked
questions.

106
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:09,000
And where do we get all of this
information to learn about what a company
actually does?

107
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Of course, we go to the company website
itself.

108
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
So we go to the company website.

109
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:22,000
We also go to the investor relations
website of the company.

110
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:28,000
And from these sources, we learn about
what the company actually does.

111
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:33,000
And we increase our qualitative
understanding of the company.

112
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:45,000
When we want to start sourcing our key
performance indicators, we move from the
company website to the investor relations
website.

113
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:55,000
And we start looking for sources where we
can locate key performance indicators and
the management operating plan.

114
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:56,000
We find on the website.

115
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:02,000
We find on the website for investor
relations, an area called events and
presentations.

116
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:15,000
And these are generally conferences or
events where the company itself is
pitching investors to invest in the
business.

117
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:31,000
So these events are generally attended by
not only investors, but also investment
bank analysts that report on the stock and
also have research models and ratings and
price targets.

118
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:50,000
What we also tend to find in this area is
presentations where the company is
updating shareholders with updates
between, so updates on the business
between earnings releases.

119
00:20:50,000 --> 00:21:09,000
So when we are looking to source key
performance indicators or a company, any
company, we always look to the investor
relations website and we navigate towards
events and presentations.

120
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:25,000
And we seek to source clues of key
performance indicators, evidence of key
performance indicators and updates to
shareholders on the business so we can
build out our KPI list.

121
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:30,000
So we went through this process for you
here in this example with Zendesk.

122
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:41,000
We clicked into presentations and what we
found was is a list of shareholder letters
with updates on the business.

123
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:57,000
So what you will find is with all
companies on investor relations websites
is that company events and presentations
provide us with an insight into what
management are focused on.

124
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:15,000
And this is great for us in identifying
what metrics other than say revenue and
earnings the company actually focuses on.

125
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:22,000
These are the drivers of the business and
they infer the management operating plan.

126
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:38,000
In these presentations guys, management is
literally telling you in plain English
what the drivers of their business are.

127
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:45,000
So there is literally no excuse not to do
the work.

128
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:59,000
It's a few clicks away to get to sources
where you can actually start listing the
key performance indicators in the
qualitative assessment for any company out
there.

129
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:08,000
You've just got to know where to look and
you have to go through your processes and
you have to interpret the information.

130
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:13,000
And of course, this requires a bit of
practice.

131
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:26,000
But over time, you're going to get quicker
at this process and you're going to get
better at interpreting the qualitative
information, the KPIs and the management
operating plan.

132
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:36,000
So what we did here with this example with
Zendesk, we clicked into the last publicly
available shareholder letter.

133
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
And this is what we found.

134
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:50,000
So firstly, we found in the opening slide
that $1 billion of revenue, the top line
target was the target for the last four
years.

135
00:23:50,000 --> 00:24:06,000
And Zendesk achieved it by hitting $1.03
billion in 2020, which was revenue growth
of 26% versus revenue in 2019.

136
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:15,000
Now, remember, this is a financial metric
and our primary concern here is
qualitative, not quantitative information.

137
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:27,000
However, of course, when we go through
presentations and shareholder letters,
there are going to be quantitative
elements that we do take note of.

138
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:42,000
Now, in this opening of the last
shareholder letter that's available, they
admit that towards the end of the year,
the business conditions were much stronger
than the beginning of the year because of
COVID-19.

139
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:54,000
So they're implying that there's a V shape
in earnings or 2020 instead of an expected
linear progression, which they didn't
expect.

140
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:59,000
So they're ending the year in a strong
growth position.

141
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:08,000
So we just take note of these things and
we keep scrolling through the presentation
and keep picking up on important
information.

142
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:13,000
So further into the presentation, we get
to more financial metrics.

143
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:22,000
So here we're being told by management of
a target to triple their revenue within
five years.

144
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:33,000
And we are also given the official
management guidance for the next quarter
Q1 and the full year ahead.

145
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:45,000
So this again is financial metrics, but
it's important information that we take
note of because we're building a context
in which the KPIs of the company exist.

146
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:50,000
It's the management operating plan and the
KPIs that are driving these numbers.

147
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:53,000
They are behind these numbers.

148
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:57,000
So we continue scrolling through the
presentation.

149
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:01,000
We continue taking notes of important
information.

150
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:18,000
We continue with our qualitative
assessment and we get to the next piece of
importance information in the shareholder
letter where we have several metrics that
must be important to the company.

151
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:23,000
It must be important to the management
operating plan and investors.

152
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:32,000
Those being ticket volume, gross bookings,
quarterly new business and expansion
bookings.

153
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:44,000
Remember guys, in these presentations, in
these updates to shareholders, management
are literally telling you in English what
is important to the business.

154
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:52,000
So in this case, we have our KPIs indexed
new tickets and indexed bookings.

155
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
So new business and expansion business.

156
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:58,000
And we take note that it's close to 60%.

157
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:03,000
So both measures are showing a rate of
growth of 60%.

158
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:20,000
We continue to dig deeper, we continue to
make notes on KPIs and we get to further
slides which show us more qualitative
information that we take note of.

159
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:31,000
It seems that it seems that the company is
now shifting its pricing plans from
individual products, plans and add-ons to
an aggregated solution.

160
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:37,000
And pricing is on a per agent or
subscription basis per month.

161
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:49,000
