345
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:39,000
So remember the context that we're in
here.

346
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:42,000
We have our quant discovery phase.

347
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:44,000
We have our quant evidence phase.

348
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:46,000
And we have our qualitative phase.

349
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:47,000
And we have our qualitative phase.

350
00:51:47,000 --> 00:52:03,000
In our quant discovery phase, we are
utilizing forward looking valuation to
identify or discover positive and negative
outliers and everything else in between
like turnarounds or potential value traps.

351
00:52:03,000 --> 00:52:04,000
In the quant evidence phase.

352
00:52:04,000 --> 00:52:06,000
In the quant evidence phase.

353
00:52:06,000 --> 00:52:15,000
We are looking to backwards looking
financial statements to provide evidence
to support valuation.

354
00:52:15,000 --> 00:52:16,000
And then we move into the qualitative
phase.

355
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:28,000
And then we move into the qualitative
phase where we are looking at backwards
and forwards looking KPIs and inferring a
management operating plan.

356
00:52:28,000 --> 00:52:31,000
And we are looking at the drivers of the
business.

357
00:52:31,000 --> 00:52:35,000
The key drivers of the business, which are
the KPIs.

358
00:52:35,000 --> 00:52:36,000
Do they provide evidence to support
valuation?

359
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:40,000
Do they provide evidence to support
valuation?

360
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:50,000
And within that qualitative assessment, we
are always remembering that we operate in
the framework where we identify KPIs.

361
00:52:50,000 --> 00:52:53,000
This infers the management operating plan.

362
00:52:53,000 --> 00:53:05,000
And the MOP and the KPIs drive the
quantitative results, the financial
metrics like revenue and earnings.

363
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:10,000
They drive the financial quantitative
results of the company.

364
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:21,000
So beneath those results, revenue and
earnings, there is always something that
creates that result.

365
00:53:21,000 --> 00:53:27,000
There is a business and this business is
driven by the management operating plan.

366
00:53:27,000 --> 00:53:31,000
And it's measured by key performance
indicators.

367
00:53:31,000 --> 00:53:35,000
So we need to keep asking ourselves the
questions.

368
00:53:35,000 --> 00:53:40,000
Why is earnings growth or revenue growth
so good or bad?

369
00:53:41,000 --> 00:53:46,000
Why does the market value the company in
the way that it does?

370
00:53:46,000 --> 00:53:53,000
Does the stock deserve to be on its
assigned premium or discount?

371
00:53:54,000 --> 00:54:02,000
Will the company continue to report good
numbers, beating expectations in revenue
and earnings?

372
00:54:02,000 --> 00:54:09,000
Or, in the case of a negative outlier,
continue to report bad numbers, missing
expectations?

373
00:54:09,000 --> 00:54:27,000
Based on our assessment of the KPIs, is it
likely, is it probable that because of the
KPIs that the company is going to continue
to report good numbers and beat
expectations?

374
00:54:27,000 --> 00:54:38,000
Or, probable in a negative outlier context
that the company is going to report bad
numbers and missing expectations?

375
00:54:38,000 --> 00:54:40,000
Is it high probability?

376
00:54:40,000 --> 00:54:54,000
And also, are there any additional
catalysts to earnings day that will get
the stock moving higher or lower in
between earnings reports?

377
00:54:54,000 --> 00:54:59,000
That's something we're going to move on to
soon when we look at catalysts.

378
00:54:59,000 --> 00:55:05,000
So we keep asking ourselves these
questions throughout our qualitative
assessment.

379
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:12,000
We first, obviously, identify KPIs and
then we infer the management operating
plan.

380
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:15,000
We know this is how companies measure
themselves.

381
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:20,000
This is how they get to their own guidance
for numbers.

382
00:55:20,000 --> 00:55:29,000
So how they get to their guidance when
they're forecasting and updating their
revenue and earnings estimates for
analysts.

383
00:55:29,000 --> 00:55:42,000
And it's also how analysts measure the
company's performance to enable them to
forecast revenue and earnings outcomes
and, therefore, the analyst expectations.

384
00:55:42,000 --> 00:56:01,000
If we have any positive or negative
changes in the KPIs, that's going to mean
a change in expectations of future revenue
and earnings forecasts and other
operational metrics.

385
00:56:01,000 --> 00:56:16,000
The stock itself will immediately move on
KPI news and analysts will follow by
upgrading or downgrading their ratings and
price targets.

386
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:32,000
This is why KPIs are so important because
the business and how it's measured moves
before the actual results that are
reported, the revenue and earnings on
earnings day.

387
00:56:32,000 --> 00:56:47,000
So we have to identify KPIs, we have to
infer the management operating plan, and
we have to stay on top of anything that
can change those KPIs.

388
00:56:47,000 --> 00:56:55,000
So this brings us now to the end of our
first step in the qualitative processing
phase.

389
00:56:55,000 --> 00:57:04,000
Let's go straight in to the next video
into the second part of the qualitative
processing phase.

390
00:57:04,000 --> 00:57:05,000
Thank you.
